WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.400 --> 00:00:07.300
The lining of your small intestine is a single layer of cells.

2
00:00:07.800 --> 00:00:12.100
Yet it moves glucose one way, from the gut into the blood.

3
00:00:12.880 --> 00:00:17.360
At the same time, it keeps the gut's contents from seeping in between the cells.

4
00:00:18.180 --> 00:00:20.200
How can one thin sheet do both?

5
00:00:20.980 --> 00:00:27.320
It can only if each cell knows which way is up, and the gaps between cells are sealed.

6
00:00:27.583 --> 00:00:30.383
Each cell in the sheet has two faces.

7
00:00:31.383 --> 00:00:33.543
The apical face looks into the gut.

8
00:00:34.363 --> 00:00:39.723
The basolateral face looks at the neighbouring cells, and below them, a thin mat

9
00:00:39.723 --> 00:00:41.343
called the basement membrane.

10
00:00:42.483 --> 00:00:45.463
The two faces carry different proteins and lipids.

11
00:00:46.303 --> 00:00:49.223
That difference is called apicobasal polarity.

12
00:00:50.117 --> 00:00:55.157
In vertebrate epithelia, junctions are stacked in a characteristic order.

13
00:00:55.877 --> 00:00:57.517
That order is not universal.

14
00:00:58.377 --> 00:01:00.477
The tight junction is the most apical.

15
00:01:01.117 --> 00:01:03.197
It seals the space between cells.

16
00:01:04.277 --> 00:01:09.317
In rat kidney tubules filled with hemoglobin, the tracer stopped at this junction.

17
00:01:10.117 --> 00:01:13.177
That placed a barrier there for a protein-sized tracer.

18
00:01:13.797 --> 00:01:16.577
It did not show that the junction is closed to ions.

19
00:01:17.550 --> 00:01:20.310
Glucose takes the route through the cell.

20
00:01:21.110 --> 00:01:28.630
On the apical face, a sodium-glucose symporter called SGLT1 carries glucose into the

21
00:01:28.630 --> 00:01:36.430
cell with sodium. For human SGLT1 in frog oocytes, two sodium ions come in with each

22
00:01:36.430 --> 00:01:40.670
sugar. The sodium runs down its gradient into the cell.

23
00:01:41.150 --> 00:01:43.630
It drags glucose in against its own.

24
00:01:44.483 --> 00:01:49.903
At the basolateral face, a sodium-independent carrier lets glucose leave.

25
00:01:50.823 --> 00:01:54.863
Glucose moves down its concentration gradient by facilitated diffusion.

26
00:01:55.723 --> 00:01:58.663
In the standard account, this carrier is GLUT2.

27
00:01:59.403 --> 00:02:05.183
The sodium-potassium pump on the same face uses ATP to push sodium out.

28
00:02:05.843 --> 00:02:07.803
It keeps cytosolic sodium low.

29
00:02:08.623 --> 00:02:12.503
Without it, the gradient that drives SGLT1 runs down.

30
00:02:13.417 --> 00:02:15.017
The polarity matters.

31
00:02:15.617 --> 00:02:22.197
Put SGLT1 on both faces, and the basolateral copies would pull glucose back in

32
00:02:22.197 --> 00:02:25.057
from the blood. That works against absorption.

33
00:02:25.717 --> 00:02:30.797
So each face carries its own transporters, and the tight junction seals the gaps.

34
00:02:31.497 --> 00:02:35.817
Together they move glucose one way and keep the gut's contents out.
